{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And He blotted out every existing thing that was upon the face of the ground</b> — After it says, \"And all flesh expired,\" and it says, \"of all that was on the dry land, they died,\" it adds and says, \"And He blotted out,\" for the bodies were blotted out and became water, as in the matter of \"and he shall blot out into the water of bitterness\" (Numbers 5:23), for the waters were boiling, according to the words of our Sages (Sanhedrin 108a). But if so, the fish too would have died. And perhaps it was as they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 32:11): \"of all that was on the dry land\" — and not the fish that are in the sea. And some say they too were included among those gathered, except that they fled to the Ocean, and in any event the fish were saved. And both of these opinions are possible, for it is conceivable that the boiling waters of the Flood mixed into the seas and heated only the upper layer of the sea, while the fish would go into the depths of the deeps and live there. Or according to the words of some who say, that the fish of the inland seas that were close to the Ocean fled there when they sensed the boiling of the water, and were saved there. And even if they all died, behold the majority of fishlife is in the Ocean, and there the Flood did not descend, as it is said (Genesis 7:12), \"and the rain was upon the earth,\" and from there the fish would return after the Flood, for from the sea all the inland seas go out and there they return to flow. And in any case the fish were saved, for they were not brought into the ark to keep seed alive, and it is said regarding the covenant (below 9:9–10), \"Behold, I establish My covenant with you and with every living creature that is with you, among the fowl, among the cattle, and among all the beasts of the earth with you, of all that go out of the ark\" — and He did not mention the fish of the sea." "<b>And they were blotted out from the earth</b> — They explained the repetition to say that their memory was forgotten, for they have no seed, and what need is there to say so after they all died? Perhaps it is on account of the birds and certain creeping things, to say that no eggs of theirs remained in any tree or under the ground, for everything was blotted out. And it is possible that the cantillation of the verse is: \"And He blotted out every existing thing that was upon the face of the ground, for from man unto beast, unto creeping thing, and unto the fowl of the heaven — they were blotted out from the earth; only Noah remained.\" And our Sages interpreted (Sanhedrin 108a): \"And He blotted out\" — in this world; \"and they were blotted out from the earth\" — from the World to Come. They made the \"earth\" mentioned here the Land of the Living, and I have already hinted at its secret (above, on 1:26 and 1:3)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:26:50.523029967Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}